{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0760b9a3c98036b20169bb39e450688191a0517baed4b9894a", "created": 1773877732, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"NCCN-Style Cancer Care Recommendations: Respected Experts and Leading Cancer Centers\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on how cancer care recommendations are created and justified in a guideline context: respected subject-matter experts develop care recommendations, and those recommendations are explicitly grounded in clinical practices associated with leading cancer centers. The structure is a tight evidence chain linking (1) leading cancer centers to (2) clinical practices/practices, which then underpin (3) care recommendations, with (4) respected experts positioned as the issuing authority for those recommendations. Overall, the community represents a clinical-guideline development ecosystem rather than an operational or adversarial network, emphasizing evidence-based, expert-supported guidance for patients.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Care recommendations are positioned as expert-developed guidance\",\"explanation\":\"The community defines CARE RECOMMENDATIONS as current, recommended approaches to cancer care, written as clear guidance statements to help patients understand up-to-date options for care in patient-facing guidelines. This frames the recommendations as both clinical and educational artifacts, intended for patient comprehension rather than only professional use. [Data: Entities (1271)]\\n\\nThe relationships reinforce that these recommendations are presented as developed and issued by RESPECTED EXPERTS, explicitly emphasizing the judgment and authority of domain specialists in the relevant field. This positioning is central to the community’s credibility model: expert authority is the direct source of the recommendations. [Data: Entities (70,1271); Relationships (2075)]\"},{\"summary\":\"A clear evidence chain ties recommendations to clinical practices at leading cancer centers\",\"explanation\":\"CARE RECOMMENDATIONS are described as being based on CLINICAL PRACTICES used at leading cancer centers, indicating that recommendations are not solely opinion-based but grounded in real-world standards of care and routine approaches. This establishes a practical basis for the guidance (what is done at top institutions) as a foundation for what is recommended to patients. [Data: Entities (1271,1272); Relationships (2078)]\\n\\nThe data includes reciprocal phrasing that CLINICAL PRACTICES inform CARE RECOMMENDATIONS, underscoring that the linkage between practices and recommendations is a primary connective tissue of this community rather than incidental context. [Data: Entities (1271,1272); Relationships (2819)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Leading cancer centers function as the origin context for practices that support recommendations\",\"explanation\":\"LEADING CANCER CENTERS are described as top-tier oncology institutions where cutting-edge treatment, research, and established best practices are developed and applied, and as key sources of the latest research and real-world oncology practices used to shape expert recommendations. In this community, these centers are the setting in which evidence and clinical experience are generated, validated, and refined. [Data: Entities (71)]\\n\\nThe relationships explicitly link LEADING CANCER CENTERS to CLINICAL PRACTICES, stating that the practices informing recommendations originate from or are associated with leading cancer centers and serve as the basis for the recommendations. This formalizes the centers’ role as the institutional anchor for the practice-based evidence used in guideline recommendations. [Data: Entities (71,1272); Relationships (2076)]\"},{\"summary\":\"‘Practices’ and ‘clinical practices’ operate as closely related concepts tied to leading centers\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes both PRACTICES and CLINICAL PRACTICES as entities, each described as representing standards, methods, and routine care approaches used at leading cancer centers and serving as foundational basis for recommendations in guidelines. While distinct labels are used, both concepts function similarly as the practice-based substrate supporting guideline recommendations. [Data: Entities (1272,2933)]\\n\\nA direct relationship ties PRACTICES specifically to LEADING CANCER CENTERS, reinforcing that the relevant practices referenced in the community are those derived from or characteristic of top oncology institutions. This strengthens the notion that the recommendations’ grounding is institutionally situated rather than generic. [Data: Entities (2933,71); Relationships (5505)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Respected experts are described as the mechanism translating evidence and practices into patient-facing guidance\",\"explanation\":\"RESPECTED EXPERTS are defined as subject-matter specialists in cancer care who develop evidence-based care recommendations presented in NCCN Guidelines and reflected in patient-oriented guideline versions, drawing on latest research and established best practices from leading cancer centers. This describes experts as the translation layer between research/practice and clear clinical guidance for patients. [Data: Entities (70)]\\n\\nThe relationship between RESPECTED EXPERTS and CARE RECOMMENDATIONS further formalizes this role: recommendations are presented as developed and issued by these experts, emphasizing authority and domain judgment as integral to guideline development. [Data: Entities (70,1271); Relationships (2075)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The network is governance/quality oriented rather than indicating legal or adversarial risk\",\"explanation\":\"All entities and relationships in the community describe guideline development and evidentiary grounding (experts, recommendations, clinical practices, and leading cancer centers). There are n